"""Multi-turn batch B: comparison shopping with evolving criteria, planning with revisions, money/maths in context, scenario tracking."""


def add(d, S):
    # ---- comparison shopping with evolving criteria ----
    d("compare_evolving", "I'm choosing between two phone plans. Plan A is 25 a month with 10 GB. Plan B is 18 a month with 5 GB.", "On price alone, B is cheaper by 7 a month (25 - 18). Over a year that's 84 less (300 versus 216). A gives double the data. How much data do you use?", "About 6 GB.", "Then B's 5 GB would be short by roughly 1 GB each month, which could mean extra charges or slow speeds. A covers you. Do you know what overage costs on B?", "B charges 4 per extra GB.", "Using 6 GB on B would cost 18 + 4 = 22 a month, still 3 cheaper than A. But if you sometimes use 8 GB, B costs 18 + 12 = 30, which is more than A's 25. So it depends on how steady your usage is.", "It varies between 5 and 8.", "At 8 GB B costs 30 and at 7 GB it costs 26, both above A's 25. Only in months at 5 or 6 GB is B cheaper. With a range up to 8 GB, A is the safer choice and probably cheaper over the year.", sys=S[0])
    d("compare_evolving", "I need a car for commuting 450 km a week. Car A uses 6 litres per 100 km, car B uses 8.", "Weekly fuel: A uses 450 x 6 / 100 = 27 litres; B uses 450 x 8 / 100 = 36 litres. A saves 9 litres a week. What does fuel cost where you live?", "1.80 per litre.", "A costs 27 x 1.80 = 48.60 a week and B costs 36 x 1.80 = 64.80, a difference of 16.20 a week. Over 50 weeks that's about 810.", "B is 2,500 cheaper to buy.", "Then the fuel saving takes 2,500 / 810 = 3.09 years, roughly three years, to repay A's higher price. If you keep the car for longer than that, A is cheaper overall (ignoring insurance, tax and repairs).", "I'd keep it about five years.", "Over five years A saves about 4,050 in fuel versus B's 2,500 lower price, so A comes out roughly 1,550 ahead on those two items. Check insurance and servicing costs too, since they can change the result.")
    d("compare_evolving", "Is it better to pay 12 a month for an app or 120 for a year?", "Monthly costs 12 x 12 = 144 a year, so the annual plan saves 24, about 17%.", "What if I only use it for 8 months?", "Eight months at 12 is 96, which is cheaper than 120. The annual plan only wins if you'd use it for more than 10 months (120 / 12 = 10).", "I might use it 10 months.", "At exactly 10 months, both cost 120. So it's a tie, but the annual plan commits you for the full 12 months while monthly lets you cancel. If you're unsure, choose monthly.", "What if the annual plan has a 14-day refund?", "That reduces the risk: you can try it, and refund within 14 days if you dislike it. But the refund doesn't help if you stop using it after month 4. Monthly is still the flexible choice unless you're confident you'll keep it for 10 months or longer.")
    d("compare_evolving", "I'm choosing a laptop bag. Option 1 costs 45, waterproof, 1 kg. Option 2 costs 70, not waterproof, 0.6 kg.", "Option 1 is 25 cheaper (70 - 45) and waterproof, but 0.4 kg heavier. Do you walk a lot with it?", "I cycle to work in all weather.", "Then waterproofing matters most, since water damage to a laptop is costly. Option 1 fits. The extra 0.4 kg on a bike is noticeable but not large.", "What if I add a rain cover to option 2 for 12?", "Option 2 plus cover costs 82, which is 37 more than Option 1. You'd get 0.4 kg less weight, but a cover can slip, blow off or be forgotten. Waterproof bags are usually the more reliable choice for daily cycling in the wet.", "I'll take option 1.", "Good choice for your commute. Pack the laptop in a padded sleeve inside the bag as well, because waterproof bags still need protection against knocks.")
    d("compare_evolving", "Help me choose between renting a flat for 950 a month and buying a flat with a mortgage payment of 1,100.", "Monthly, the mortgage is 150 higher (1,100 - 950) which is 1,800 a year. But buying also adds taxes, insurance, maintenance and the deposit. How long do you plan to stay?", "Maybe 8 years.", "Over 8 years, rent totals 950 x 96 = 91,200 (ignoring rent rises). The mortgage payments total 1,100 x 96 = 105,600. The extra 14,400 buys equity in the property, but part of each payment is interest. The question is whether the property's value and your equity make up the difference.", "Maintenance would be around 1,500 a year.", "Then add 12,000 over 8 years, bringing the cost of owning to about 117,600 versus 91,200 renting, a gap of 26,400. Buying pays off only if the equity you build plus any price gain is larger than that gap plus your deposit's lost return.", "I can't tell without knowing the value gain. Is that right?", "Yes. I can't predict prices. A useful test is to ask how much the flat must rise for you to break even. That depends on your loan balance after 8 years and selling costs, which I can calculate if you share the loan size, rate and the price.")

    # ---- planning with revisions ----
    d("plan_revision", "Plan a 5-day meal prep for one person, vegetarian, around 600 calories per dinner.", "Rotate three dinners: lentil bolognese with wholegrain pasta (~600), chickpea and spinach curry with rice (~600), and tofu stir-fry with noodles (~600). Cook two batches on Sunday and one midweek.", "I don't like tofu.", "Swap the tofu stir-fry for a black bean and sweet potato chilli with a small portion of rice, around 600 calories as well.", "And I'm out of rice.", "Serve the chilli with a baked potato or a slice of wholegrain bread. The curry will need another base too: use quinoa, couscous or potatoes instead of rice.", "Can you give me the final plan for Monday to Friday?", "Monday: lentil bolognese with pasta. Tuesday: black bean and sweet potato chilli with a baked potato. Wednesday: chickpea and spinach curry with couscous. Thursday: lentil bolognese again. Friday: chilli with a baked potato. Calories are approximate, around 600 each. This keeps your no-tofu and no-rice conditions. Cook the bolognese and the chilli in large batches, and the curry fresh on Wednesday.")
    d("plan_revision", "I want to read 12 books in a year. Make a plan.", "That's one book a month. If a book has about 300 pages, that's about 10 pages a day. Choose 12 titles in advance, mixing long and short ones.", "I read slowly. Maybe 15 pages in 30 minutes.", "At 15 pages per 30 minutes, 10 pages take 20 minutes a day. A 300-page book takes about 10 hours, so 12 books need around 120 hours: 20 minutes a day gives 120 hours a year (20 x 365 / 60 = 121.7). It works.", "Some of my books are 500 pages.", "A 500-page book takes about 16.7 hours at your pace. If three of the twelve are that long, the total becomes 9 x 10 + 3 x 16.7 = 140 hours, roughly 23 minutes a day. Still doable; or swap a long book for a shorter one.", "OK. So how long per day?", "About 25 minutes a day gives you a small buffer (25 x 365 / 60 = 152 hours). Read in one regular slot, like before bed.")
    d("plan_revision", "I'm organising a team offsite for 20 people with a budget of 4,000.", "That's 200 per person. Typical costs are venue, food, transport and activities. What do you expect to pay for the venue?", "The venue is 1,200.", "That leaves 2,800, or 140 per person, for everything else. Food for a day is typically 30 to 45 per person, so 600 to 900 for 20.", "Lunch and dinner at 40 per person.", "Meals total 40 x 20 = 800, leaving 2,000. That's 100 per person for transport and activities.", "Transport by coach is 700.", "Then 1,300 remains for activities (65 per person). A facilitator, a workshop or an outdoor activity could fit. Total spend would be 1,200 + 800 + 700 + 1,300 = 4,000.", "Add a 10% contingency, please.", "A 10% contingency of 4,000 is 400. Reduce the activities budget to 900 to keep the total at 4,000: 1,200 + 800 + 700 + 900 + 400 = 4,000.")
    d("plan_revision", "I need to move out in 6 weeks. Make a checklist.", "Week 6: give notice, set a budget, list what you'll take. Week 5: find boxes, start packing seasonal items. Week 4: book a van or movers, redirect mail. Week 3: pack most rooms. Week 2: clean, confirm utilities. Week 1: final pack, handover keys, photos of the empty flat.", "I won't have a car or van. Movers are too expensive.", "Then plan to move in several trips by public transport with suitcases for essentials, and ask friends for help or rent a van by the hour for one day only. Sell or donate large items you don't need.", "My new place is only 2 km away.", "At 2 km, a few trips on foot or by bike with a trolley or cargo bike is realistic. A small van for two hours would be inexpensive. So week 4: book a van for 2 hours, not movers.", "Update the checklist please.", "Updated: Week 6: give notice, list what to take, sell what you won't. Week 5: collect boxes, pack seasonal items, ask friends about moving day. Week 4: book a 2-hour van for moving day, redirect mail. Week 3: pack most rooms. Week 2: clean, set up utilities at the new place. Week 1: final packing, move large items with the van, photograph the empty flat, hand over keys.")
    d("plan_revision", "I want to save 2,400 over 8 months.", "That's 2,400 / 8 = 300 a month.", "I saved 200 in month 1 and 200 in month 2.", "After two months you have 400, short of the 600 plan by 200. Remaining: 2,000 over 6 months, which is 333.33 a month.", "I can do 350 a month from now on.", "6 x 350 = 2,100, so you'd finish with 400 + 2,100 = 2,500, which is 100 above the goal. You could stop one month early at 2,400: after 5 more months at 350 you'd have 400 + 1,750 = 2,150, and the sixth month needs only 250.", "Okay, so the sixth month I pay only 250?", "Yes. Months 3 to 7 at 350 (1,750), then month 8 at 250, brings you to 400 + 1,750 + 250 = 2,400 exactly.")
    d("plan_revision", "Plan my Saturday: gym, groceries, laundry, and coffee with a friend at 3 pm.", "A workable order: gym in the morning (9:30), groceries on the way back, put laundry on when you get home, then relax and shower. Coffee at 3 pm. Laundry finishes in time to hang or dry before you go out.", "The gym closes at noon on Saturdays.", "Fine, a 9:30 session ends well before noon. Is there a time you can't do anything?", "I can't leave before 9.", "Then leave at 9:00 for the gym, work out until about 10:30, do groceries at 11, home by 12 with laundry going, lunch, then coffee at 3. That fits within the gym's hours and your 9:00 start.", "Friend moved coffee to 1 pm.", "Then compress: gym 9:00 to 10:15, groceries 10:30 to 11:15, home at 11:30, start laundry, quick lunch, leave for coffee at 12:40. Move the laundry folding to the evening.")

    # ---- money and maths in context ----
    d("money_context", "I earn 3,200 a month after tax. Rent is 1,100 and bills are 250.", "Fixed costs are 1,100 + 250 = 1,350, leaving 1,850 for food, transport, saving and everything else.", "Food is about 400 and transport 120.", "That's 520, so 1,850 - 520 = 1,330 remains.", "I want to save 20% of income.", "20% of 3,200 is 640, so the remainder is 1,330 - 640 = 690 for spending on everything else.", "Is 690 realistic?", "It depends on your lifestyle, but 690 a month, around 23 a day, is workable for most people if you don't have debt payments. If it feels tight, reduce savings to 15% (480), which leaves 850 to spend.")
    d("money_context", "I have a credit card balance of 2,000 with 24% annual interest.", "Annual interest on 2,000 at 24% is 480, which is roughly 40 a month (2% a month).", "If I pay 200 a month, how long?", "About 12 months. The first payment of 200 covers 40 of interest and reduces the balance by 160, and each later payment reduces it a bit more as interest falls. Month by month at 2% interest, the balance reaches zero in the 12th payment, which is a small final one.", "And how much interest would I pay?", "Roughly 254 in total, if the card charges exactly 2% a month on the remaining balance. The exact figure depends on how your card calculates interest and when payments post.", "What if I pay 400 a month?", "It takes 6 payments (the last one is smaller), and the interest is about 129. Paying 400 instead of 200 saves roughly 125 in interest and clears the debt six months sooner, because interest is charged on what remains.")
    d("money_context", "My rent is going up from 800 to 860.", "That's an increase of 60, or 7.5% (60 / 800).", "What does that cost me per year?", "60 x 12 = 720 extra per year.", "My salary goes up 3%. I earn 2,500.", "A 3% rise on 2,500 is 75 more per month, so 2,575. That covers the 60 rent increase with 15 to spare.", "So I'm slightly better off?", "Yes, by about 15 a month before tax effects. Note that if the 2,500 is gross and the rise is taxed, the net gain may be less than 75, so the surplus could be smaller or gone. If 2,500 is after tax, you're up by 15.")
    d("money_context", "A jacket costs 120. It's 25% off, then I have an extra 10% off coupon.", "25% off 120 is 90. Then 10% off 90 is 81. So the price is 81.", "My friend says that's the same as 35% off.", "Not quite. 35% off 120 would be 78. Successive discounts multiply: 0.75 x 0.90 = 0.675, so the total discount is 32.5%, which gives 81.", "What if the coupon applied first?", "The order doesn't matter: 120 x 0.90 = 108, then x 0.75 = 81. Same result, because multiplication is commutative.", "What if it's 10% off the original price instead?", "Then you'd take 25% (30) and 10% (12) off the original 120, a total of 42 off, so you pay 78. That is the same as 35% off. The earlier case, where the second discount applies to the reduced price, gives a smaller saving.")
    d("money_context", "I invest 200 a month at an average of 6% a year. How much after 10 years?", "Using monthly compounding at 0.5% a month for 120 months, the future value is roughly 32,800. Contributions total 24,000, so growth is about 8,800. Actual returns vary and are not guaranteed.", "What if I start 5 years later and invest 300?", "Starting later gives you 5 years: 300 a month at 0.5% for 60 months comes to about 20,900, with 18,000 contributed. That is well below the 10-year result, even with a higher monthly amount.", "So starting early matters?", "Yes. Compare: 10 years of 200 gives about 32,800 and 5 years of 300 gives about 20,900. The earlier money has more time to grow. These figures assume a steady 6% return, and real markets are bumpy.")
    d("money_context", "My electricity bill was 84 in January, 78 in February, and 90 in March.", "The total is 84 + 78 + 90 = 252, an average of 84 a month.", "April's bill is 66. What's the new average?", "(252 + 66) / 4 = 318 / 4 = 79.50.", "Which month was the biggest increase over the month before?", "January to February fell by 6, February to March rose by 12, March to April fell by 24. The only increase was Feb to March, +12.", "If May is the same as April, what's the 5-month average?", "(318 + 66) / 5 = 384 / 5 = 76.80.")

    # ---- work / writing coaching across turns ----
    d("writing_coach", "Help me with my cover letter opening. I'm applying for a marketing role.", "A strong opening names the role, shows enthusiasm and gives one specific reason you fit. For example: \"I'm excited to apply for the Marketing Coordinator role at Brightwell. I've spent three years growing small-business social channels, and I'd like to bring that to your team.\" Tell me your real background and I'll tailor it.", "I ran social media for a bakery for two years. Grew followers from 800 to 5,000.", "That is a concrete result. \"I'm excited to apply for the Marketing Coordinator role at Brightwell. Over two years managing social media for a local bakery, I grew its following from 800 to 5,000, and I'd like to bring the same energy to your campaigns.\" That growth is 4,200 followers, or about 6.25 times the start.", "It sounds a bit bragging.", "Soften it by focusing on what you learned and the team: \"I'm excited to apply for the Marketing Coordinator role at Brightwell. Running social media for a local bakery for two years taught me how to build an audience, from 800 to 5,000 followers, and I'm keen to learn from and contribute to your team.\"", "Good. What should the second paragraph do?", "Pick one or two skills from the job advert and connect each to a concrete example from your bakery work, such as a campaign that increased orders. Because I haven't seen the advert, tell me the key requirements and I'll help match them.")
    d("writing_coach", "Edit this sentence: 'The team, which was very tired, completed the project on time, however they were disappointed.'", "A cleaner version: \"The team, tired but on schedule, completed the project; even so, they were disappointed.\" The main fix is that 'however' joining two clauses needs a semicolon before it, not a comma.", "Keep 'very tired' though.", "Then: \"The team, very tired, completed the project on time; however, they were disappointed.\" I used a semicolon before 'however' and a comma after it.", "Could you make it more dramatic?", "\"Exhausted but on schedule, the team finished the project, and still they felt let down.\" I changed 'very tired' to 'exhausted', which you asked me to keep in a weaker form, so here is another one that keeps your phrase: \"Very tired, the team completed the project on time, and yet they were bitterly disappointed.\"")
    d("writing_coach", "I need a toast for my sister's graduation, 3 sentences.", "\"To my sister, who made all those late nights look easy. We're so proud of you today. Here's to everything you'll do next.\"", "She studied nursing. Mention that.", "\"To my sister, who spent three years learning how to care for others and never stopped caring for us. We're so proud of you today. Here's to the patients who will be lucky to have you.\" I said 'three years' as a guess; use the real length of her course.", "Four years actually.", "\"To my sister, who spent four years learning how to care for others and never stopped caring for us. We're so proud of you today. Here's to the patients who will be lucky to have you.\"")
    d("writing_coach", "Can you shorten this? 'I am writing to let you know that I will be unable to attend the meeting that is scheduled for tomorrow due to the fact that I have a doctor's appointment.'", "\"I can't attend tomorrow's meeting because I have a doctor's appointment.\" That is 11 words instead of 35.", "A bit blunt. Add an apology.", "\"I'm sorry, but I can't attend tomorrow's meeting because I have a doctor's appointment.\"", "And offer to catch up afterwards.", "\"I'm sorry, but I can't attend tomorrow's meeting because I have a doctor's appointment. Could I catch up with you afterwards, or read the notes?\"")
    d("writing_coach", "Suggest a title for a blog post about learning to cook as an adult.", "\"Burnt Toast and Better Dinners: Learning to Cook at 30.\" Other options: \"I Couldn't Boil an Egg, Then I Learned\" and \"Adult Cooking 101.\"", "I'm 42, not 30.", "Then: \"Burnt Toast and Better Dinners: Learning to Cook at 42.\" Or avoid the number: \"Learning to Cook Later in Life.\"", "Does a number in the title help?", "Often yes: a specific number can feel more personal and curious, and it signals who the post is for. Using your real age makes it honest and distinct. If you want a broader audience, drop the number.")
    d("writing_coach", "Write a one-line LinkedIn summary for a data analyst.", "\"Data analyst who turns messy spreadsheets into clear decisions, using SQL, Python and dashboards.\"", "I don't use Python, I use R.", "\"Data analyst who turns messy spreadsheets into clear decisions, using SQL, R and dashboards.\"", "Add that I work in healthcare.", "\"Healthcare data analyst who turns messy records into clear decisions, using SQL, R and dashboards.\" If your data involves patients, keep any examples you share free of identifying details.")

    # ---- topic return and tracking across detours ----
    d("detour_return", "I want to learn to bake bread. Where do I start?", "Start with a simple no-knead loaf: flour, water, salt, yeast, a long rest and a hot Dutch oven. It teaches fermentation without much effort.", "Quick question first, what's a Dutch oven exactly?", "A heavy, lidded pot (often cast iron) that holds heat well. It traps steam around the loaf and makes a crisp crust.", "Okay. I don't have one. Can I still bake the loaf?", "Yes. Use a heavy covered casserole dish, or bake on a preheated tray with a tray of water on the oven floor for steam. The crust will be slightly less crisp.", "Going back to your first answer: how long is the rest?", "For a no-knead loaf, the first rise is usually 12 to 18 hours at room temperature, then 1 to 2 hours for a second rise before baking.")
    d("detour_return", "Help me choose a gift for my mum. She likes gardening.", "Ideas: quality secateurs, a kneeling pad, a set of heirloom seeds, a plant she doesn't have, or a gardening book. What's your budget?", "Around 40. By the way, how do I wrap a gift without tape?", "Use a fabric wrap (furoshiki style): place the gift diagonally on a square of cloth, fold the corners to the middle, and tie them in a knot. Ribbon or string also works.", "That's neat. Back to the gift: does she need tools if she already gardens a lot?", "A good pair of secateurs is a smart gift for a keen gardener, because quality tools wear out and she'll use them often. At around 40 you can find reliable bypass secateurs. A fabric wrap would suit the gift, too.")
    d("detour_return", "I'm trying to decide on a major: economics or psychology.", "What draws you to each? Different careers, topics, or kinds of work?", "Psychology interests me more, but economics pays better.", "That's common. Is there a career you're considering in each?", "Wait, quick one: how many credits do I need to graduate?", "That varies by institution, often around 120 credits for a bachelor's degree in the US. Check your university's requirements.", "OK, so about the major. Psychology could lead to research or counselling, economics to finance.", "Then the next step is to test each cheaply: take an introductory course in both, speak to people in jobs you're considering, and look at average pay for roles you'd actually want. Pay differences between majors are real, but they shrink when you do work you're good at and enjoy.")

    # ---- Cooking / hobbies tracking across turns ----
    d("recipe_scale", "A pancake recipe for 4 uses 300 g flour, 2 eggs and 500 ml milk. Scale it for 6.", "Multiply by 1.5: 450 g flour, 3 eggs, 750 ml milk.", "And for 10?", "Multiply by 2.5: 750 g flour, 5 eggs, 1,250 ml (1.25 litres) milk.", "I only have 4 eggs.", "With 4 eggs you can make a batch for 8 (multiply by 2): 600 g flour, 4 eggs, 1,000 ml milk. For 10 you'd be one egg short, so add a bit of extra milk or a banana to bind, or make fewer pancakes.", "How many eggs per person is that?", "The recipe uses 2 eggs for 4 people, which is half an egg per person. For 8 people that's 4 eggs, matching the batch above.")
    d("recipe_scale", "I'm painting a room: four walls, 4 m x 3 m, ceiling height 2.5 m. How much paint?", "Wall area: the perimeter is 2 x (4 + 3) = 14 m, times 2.5 m high gives 35 m2.", "There's one door of 2 m2 and a window of 1.5 m2.", "Subtract them: 35 - 3.5 = 31.5 m2 of wall.", "Two coats. Paint covers 10 m2 per litre.", "Two coats of 31.5 m2 is 63 m2, so you need 6.3 litres. Buy 7 litres, or a 5-litre and a 2.5-litre tin.", "Does that include the ceiling?", "No. The ceiling would add 4 x 3 = 12 m2, or 24 m2 for two coats, another 2.4 litres. Together that is about 8.7 litres, so around 9 litres in total.")
    d("recipe_scale", "I want to make 1.5 litres of soup. My recipe makes 600 ml with 200 g vegetables and 400 ml stock.", "To go from 600 ml to 1,500 ml, multiply by 2.5: 500 g vegetables and 1,000 ml stock.", "I only have 400 g vegetables.", "Then you'd make 400 / 500 x 1.5 litres = 1.2 litres with 800 ml of stock, scaling everything down by the same ratio. Or add beans or potatoes to bulk it up.", "I have a can of beans (about 240 g).", "Adding the beans brings you to 640 g of solids, more than the 500 g needed, so you can make the full 1.5 litres: use your 400 g of vegetables plus about 100 g of beans, and keep the rest of the can for another day. Use 1,000 ml of stock.")

    # ---- health-general (non-diagnostic) ----
    d("health_general", "I want to build a habit of drinking more water. I currently have about 3 glasses a day.", "A common target is around 8 glasses (2 litres), though needs vary. Add one glass at a time: morning, with each meal, and mid-afternoon.", "I forget, I'm busy.", "Link it to routines: a glass after brushing your teeth, one with each meal, and a bottle on your desk. Use a bottle with markings, or a phone reminder at set times.", "How many glasses is a 1.5 litre bottle?", "If a glass is 250 ml, a 1.5 litre bottle is 6 glasses (1,500 / 250). Two more glasses make 8, or 2 litres.", "I'll try 2 bottles a day.", "Two 1.5-litre bottles are 3 litres, which is more than the 2-litre target, and more than most people need. Aim for one bottle plus 2 glasses, or 2 litres. If you exercise or it's hot, you may need more, and a doctor can advise if you have a medical condition.")
    d("health_general", "I sit for 9 hours a day at work. How do I counter that?", "Stand up every 30 to 60 minutes, take short walks, and add a daily walk of 20 to 30 minutes. Stretch the hips, chest and back. Strength training twice a week helps too.", "I only have a 10 minute lunch break.", "Use it for a brisk walk of 10 minutes, which adds roughly 1,000 steps. Combine it with standing for a minute or two every half hour at your desk.", "How many steps is a 20 minute walk?", "Roughly 2,000 to 2,500 steps at a normal pace, depending on your stride. So two 10-minute walks add up to about the same.", "OK, 10 minutes at lunch and 10 after work.", "That's a fine start: about 2,000 steps from those two walks. Increase gradually as it becomes habit, and add standing breaks to reduce the long unbroken sitting.")
    d("health_general", "I'm starting a running programme. Week 1 is 3 runs of 20 minutes.", "Sounds fine for a beginner if you use a run-walk approach, like 1 minute running and 2 minutes walking, repeated.", "What about week 2?", "Increase the running intervals a little: 2 minutes running and 2 minutes walking, three runs again. Add rest days between runs.", "I've missed two days. Should I double up?", "No. Don't double up to catch up, as that raises injury risk. Resume where you left off, or repeat the previous week's session if you missed more than a few days.", "How many sessions will I have done after 4 weeks if I do 3 a week?", "3 x 4 = 12 sessions. If you missed two sessions, then 10.")
    d("health_general", "My sleep is bad. I go to bed at 1 and wake at 7.", "That's 6 hours in bed, which is short for most adults, who need 7 to 9. Can you move bedtime earlier or wake later?", "I must wake at 7.", "Then shift bedtime earlier in steps: 15 minutes earlier every few days until you reach about 11 p.m. for 8 hours. Dim lights and put screens away an hour before bed.", "How many days to get from 1:00 to 11:00 in 15-minute steps?", "That's 2 hours, or 8 steps of 15 minutes. If you take each step for 3 days, it takes 24 days.", "Seems long. Can I do 30 minutes each?", "Yes: 4 steps of 30 minutes, 3 days each, 12 days. Faster changes can be harder to stick to, so see how you feel.")

    # ---- travel / time zones / logistics ----
    d("logistics", "I need to schedule a call between London and New York at a time that suits both. Say it's summer and both use daylight saving.", "In summer London is on BST and New York on EDT, and London is 5 hours ahead. So 9:00 in New York is 14:00 in London.", "What about 15:00 London?", "That is 10:00 in New York.", "I'd rather avoid anything past 17:00 for London people.", "Then the call must start by 16:00 London time and end by 17:00, which is 11:00 to 12:00 in New York. The range 9:00 to 12:00 in New York works for both.", "Pick one.", "I'd suggest 15:00 London, 10:00 New York. It's mid-morning for New York and leaves London people free by 16:00.")
    d("logistics", "My flight leaves at 14:35. The airport is 45 minutes away and I want to be there 2 hours before.", "Arrive at the airport by 12:35 (14:35 minus 2 hours). With a 45 minute drive, leave home by 11:50.", "Add 20 minutes for traffic.", "Leave by 11:30.", "Parking takes about 15 minutes and then a shuttle for 10.", "Add 25 more minutes: leave by 11:05. If you want to be at check-in by 12:35, the sequence is: leave 11:05, drive 45 + 20 = 65 minutes to 12:10, park and shuttle 25 minutes to 12:35.", "Good. When should I set my alarm if I need 1 hour to get ready?", "Leave at 11:05, so you need to start getting ready by 10:05. Set the alarm for 9:50 to wake up properly.")
    d("logistics", "A conference runs from Tuesday 9:00 to Thursday 17:00. How many hours is that?", "Tuesday 9:00 to Thursday 17:00 is 2 days and 8 hours, or 56 hours of elapsed time.", "The sessions only run 9 to 17 each day.", "Then each day has 8 hours of sessions, over three days that's 24 hours.", "With one hour lunch each day, how many hours of sessions?", "Subtract 3 hours: 21 hours of actual sessions.", "I'll skip Thursday afternoon, after lunch at 13:00. How many hours do I attend?", "Tuesday and Wednesday are 7 hours each (8 minus lunch), so 14. Thursday, from 9 to 13, is 4 hours, but if lunch is within that span, it depends when lunch is. Assuming lunch is at 12:00 to 13:00, you attend 3 hours of sessions on Thursday: 14 + 3 = 17 hours.")
    d("logistics", "I have a 90 minute train. I want to work for 60 of those minutes on my laptop. The battery is at 40%.", "That depends on your laptop's consumption. A typical laptop uses 10 to 20% of battery per hour for light work. At those rates, 60 minutes would use 10 to 20%, so you'd have enough.", "Mine uses about 35% an hour with video.", "Then 60 minutes uses 35%, leaving 5% from 40%. That's cutting it close, so reduce brightness, close video, or bring a charger or power bank.", "Without video, it used 20% an hour.", "Then 60 minutes uses 20%, leaving 20%. That's safe, though the train's Wi-Fi may increase usage a little. Download what you need before you leave.")

    # ---- honesty about what the assistant cannot do mid-conversation ----
    d("honest_limits", "Remind me at 5 pm to call the dentist.", "I can't set reminders, since I have no way to send you notifications or access a clock or calendar. I can help you write the reminder text, and your phone's reminder app would do the job. Would you like a short wording?", "Yes. And add it for tomorrow instead.", "Reminder text: \"Call the dentist to book an appointment. Tomorrow 5:00 pm.\" Put that into your phone's reminders or calendar with an alarm, and set the date to tomorrow.", "Can you check that my phone did it?", "No, I can't see your phone or confirm anything on it. Check that the reminder appears in your list with the correct date and time, and consider setting a second alert 10 minutes earlier.")
    d("honest_limits", "What's the weather in Paris right now?", "I can't see live weather. I don't have internet access in this conversation. A weather service or app will give you current conditions. If you tell me the forecast, I can help you decide what to wear or plan.", "It says 12 C and rain.", "Then wear a waterproof jacket and layers; 12 C feels cool, especially in rain. Bring an umbrella and shoes that can handle puddles. If you're sightseeing, plan indoor stops like museums during the heaviest showers.", "Is that normal for April?", "Yes. Paris in April commonly has highs of 14 to 17 C and showers, so 12 C and rain on a given day is unremarkable. These are typical climate patterns rather than current data.")
    d("honest_limits", "Can you look up the cheapest flight to Rome next month?", "I can't search flights or see current prices, since I have no live access to booking sites. I can help you plan the search: which dates are flexible, which airports to compare, and how to read the fare rules.", "I'm flexible. Leaving from Manchester.", "Then try flexible-date views on a fare comparison site, compare Manchester with nearby airports, and look at midweek flights, which are often cheaper. Check Rome's two airports (Fiumicino and Ciampino) and how far each is from the city centre.", "Which day of the week is cheapest?", "Typically Tuesday, Wednesday and Saturday departures tend to be cheaper than Friday and Sunday, but it varies by route and season, and I can't confirm it for your dates. Use the site's calendar view to compare.")
    d("honest_limits", "Remember my name is Priya for next time.", "Nice to meet you, Priya. I'll use your name in this conversation, but I can't promise to remember it in a separate chat unless the app I'm running in saves it for me.", "What's my name?", "Priya, as you told me in this conversation.", "Will you remember tomorrow?", "I may not. Unless the app keeps a memory or gives me previous messages, a new conversation starts fresh. If you want continuity, you can start the next chat by telling me your name, or paste a short summary.")
